1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,455
Every tool on this computer needs a model.

2
00:00:03,455 --> 00:00:07,776
Each one used to carry its
own address, its own key,

3
00:00:07,776 --> 00:00:11,516
and its own plan for
when the internet drops.

4
00:00:11,516 --> 00:00:13,902
Fairlead is the model router.

5
00:00:13,902 --> 00:00:19,331
One local address, one key per
tool, and a trace of every request.

6
00:00:19,331 --> 00:00:21,151
Here is how Fairlead fits together.

7
00:00:21,151 --> 00:00:24,230
Fleet tools call one
address on this computer,

8
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:28,379
in the OpenAI or the Anthropic
dialect, each with its own key.

9
00:00:28,905 --> 00:00:33,013
Inside the engine, a
request is checked, routed,

10
00:00:33,013 --> 00:00:36,266
retried if a target fails, and logged.

11
00:00:36,266 --> 00:00:40,419
The log keeps the
decision, never the prompt.

12
00:00:41,119 --> 00:00:44,055
One piece makes the outbound calls.

13
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:47,763
Local models, Ollama and Windlass.

14
00:00:47,763 --> 00:00:51,085
Cloud providers, OpenAI and OpenRouter.

15
00:00:51,695 --> 00:00:54,049
Cloud keys live in Lazarette,

16
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:57,296
shared by name, and held in memory only.

17
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,700
Mark a request private, and
it never leaves this PC.

18
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,730
Planned, not built:

19
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:09,468
git hosts and databases behind the same
address, and routing by prompt difficulty.

20
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:11,578
Every request leaves a trace.

21
00:01:12,127 --> 00:01:14,040
That is the evidence. Start with the key.

22
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:18,257
It goes into Lazarette, the
encrypted vault on this machine,

23
00:01:18,257 --> 00:01:22,756
as an API credential named OPENAI
underscore API underscore KEY.

24
00:01:23,068 --> 00:01:26,029
Test credential checks it against OpenAI's

25
00:01:26,029 --> 00:01:29,061
read only endpoint
before anything uses it.

26
00:01:29,061 --> 00:01:31,830
This footage comes from the Halyard demo.

27
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:34,467
Then share it with Fairlead by name.

28
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:37,135
Fairlead sees only what you share.

29
00:01:37,912 --> 00:01:41,727
Fairlead reads it from
the vault and connects.

30
00:01:41,727 --> 00:01:45,560
That is the only place
the cloud key lives.

31
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:48,266
The Providers page. Lazarette is paired,

32
00:01:48,266 --> 00:01:51,513
and Fairlead asks it for
two names, both shared.

33
00:01:51,513 --> 00:01:53,343
Lazarette is locked right now,

34
00:01:53,343 --> 00:01:56,699
so Fairlead keeps what it
already read for eight hours.

35
00:01:57,399 --> 00:01:59,269
OpenAI. Test connection.

36
00:01:59,269 --> 00:02:04,035
Connected, with the key from
Lazarette, not typed here.

37
00:02:04,735 --> 00:02:06,817
Ollama, the local provider.

38
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:10,990
Fetch model list finds Llama
three point two, three billion,

39
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:14,190
imported from a model
file already on this PC.

40
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:17,090
Routes are the model
names your tools send.

41
00:02:17,090 --> 00:02:19,755
Smart is one target, fast is another,

42
00:02:19,755 --> 00:02:24,220
and each can hold an ordered
list that fails over to the next.

43
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,898
The local route was empty,

44
00:02:26,898 --> 00:02:30,855
so we give it a target: the
model we just installed.

45
00:02:30,855 --> 00:02:33,095
Private requests and
offline mode land here.

46
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:39,486
Auto picks a route for each request
from rules: prompt size, tools,

47
00:02:39,486 --> 00:02:44,413
images, keywords, and whether
the prompt carries a secret.

48
00:02:45,113 --> 00:02:47,428
Aliases catch hard coded names,

49
00:02:47,428 --> 00:02:50,639
so any claude or gpt
name lands on a route.

50
00:02:50,639 --> 00:02:52,070
Every tool gets its own key.

51
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:56,163
The Install button makes one and saves
it to Windows Credential Manager,

52
00:02:56,163 --> 00:02:59,971
so the tool reads it from there
and it never sits in a config file.

53
00:03:00,671 --> 00:03:02,952
Keys are shown once and
stored only as a hash.

54
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:06,902
Each can be limited by route,
by rate, and by daily tokens,

55
00:03:06,902 --> 00:03:10,316
and Revoke ends one tool
without touching the rest.

56
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:14,563
Each tool's setup is copy
and paste: the OpenAI SDK,

57
00:03:14,563 --> 00:03:18,178
curl with the privacy header,
or the Halyard profile.

58
00:03:18,178 --> 00:03:20,981
Claude Code needs just
a base URL and a token.

59
00:03:21,231 --> 00:03:24,140
Settings. Kedge mode
is the offline switch.

60
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,772
Auto goes local the moment
the cloud is out of reach.

61
00:03:27,772 --> 00:03:30,008
I will not force it on here,

62
00:03:30,008 --> 00:03:34,002
because other tools share
this Fairlead right now.

63
00:03:34,702 --> 00:03:37,339
Privacy: look for secrets in prompts,

64
00:03:37,339 --> 00:03:41,616
keep private requests on local
models, require a client key.

65
00:03:41,616 --> 00:03:46,790
Prompts are kept only if you tick content
logging, in memory, never on disk.

66
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:48,188
It is off.

67
00:03:48,888 --> 00:03:52,385
Diagnostics. The network,
and each provider:

68
00:03:52,385 --> 00:03:57,709
Ollama and OpenAI reachable, Windlass
not running, with the reason.

69
00:03:58,409 --> 00:04:02,148
Circuit breakers below keep
dead targets out of the way,

70
00:04:02,148 --> 00:04:05,820
so a failing model stops
costing every request a retry.

71
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:09,036
A real request. Model
auto, a short prompt.

72
00:04:09,036 --> 00:04:11,917
Explain shows the plan
without calling a model:

73
00:04:11,917 --> 00:04:15,901
the quick chat rule matched, and
the first choice is OpenAI mini.

74
00:04:16,601 --> 00:04:21,661
Send. OpenAI answered, and the decision
and the attempt are right there.

75
00:04:22,361 --> 00:04:25,044
Now a prompt that carries a cloud key.

76
00:04:25,044 --> 00:04:30,027
It is the example key from Amazon's
documentation, not a real credential.

77
00:04:30,027 --> 00:04:34,244
Explain says private
request, local models only.

78
00:04:34,944 --> 00:04:36,584
Send.

79
00:04:37,284 --> 00:04:40,177
It never leaves this PC. Llama
answered, and the log marks it private.

80
00:04:40,877 --> 00:04:45,241
Without a secret, tick private, which
sends the privacy header any tool can set.

81
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:48,039
Same result: it stays on this machine.

82
00:04:48,753 --> 00:04:49,936
Now another Fleet tool.

83
00:04:49,936 --> 00:04:53,193
This is Halyard, with
its own Fairlead key.

84
00:04:53,193 --> 00:04:56,414
Test model sends a real
request through Fairlead.

85
00:04:56,414 --> 00:04:59,543
Reply from auto, in under half a second.

86
00:05:00,243 --> 00:05:02,481
On the overview, the
routing map shows each tool

87
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:04,439
as a client, and the providers it reached.

88
00:05:05,139 --> 00:05:08,419
Activity lists every request with
its client, route and target.

89
00:05:08,419 --> 00:05:12,651
Halyard's is right there, next to
other demos running on this PC.

90
00:05:12,651 --> 00:05:17,199
Open one: the rule that matched,
the target and the timing.

91
00:05:17,199 --> 00:05:18,723
Prompts are not kept.

92
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:20,592
Failover is logged too.

93
00:05:20,592 --> 00:05:23,370
I sent this one from the command line,

94
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:26,367
to a route whose first model was retired.

95
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:27,970
OpenAI answered not found,

96
00:05:27,970 --> 00:05:32,469
and Fairlead moved to the next target
before any byte reached the caller.

97
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,886
Fairlead is the one address.

98
00:05:34,886 --> 00:05:38,422
Keys stay in the vault,
each tool holds one key,

99
00:05:38,422 --> 00:05:44,314
private requests stay on this PC, and
every request leaves a trace you can read.

100
00:05:44,314 --> 00:05:48,627
The demos in this series that
call a model went through it.

101
00:05:48,627 --> 00:05:53,158
Planned next: git hosts and
databases behind the same address.

102
00:05:53,158 --> 00:05:54,401
That's the watch.
